A recommendation is a category-A-recommendation and is an evidence-type-3-recommendation.
If a clinician starts an opioid-therapy for an opioid-naive-patient that has an acute-pain then the clinician should prescribe a lowest-effective-dosage.
If a clinician starts an opioid-therapy for an opioid-naive-patient that has a subacute-pain then the clinician should prescribe a lowest-effective-dosage.
If a clinician starts an opioid-therapy for an opioid-naive-patient that has a chronic-pain then the clinician should prescribe a lowest-effective-dosage.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a subacute-pain and the clinician prescribes an opioid at a dosage then the clinician should heed a caution.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a chronic-pain and the clinician prescribes an opioid at a dosage then the clinician should heed a caution.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a subacute-pain and the clinician considers a dosage-increase then the clinician should evaluate an individual-benefit and should evaluate an individual-risk.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a chronic-pain and the clinician considers a dosage-increase then the clinician should evaluate an individual-benefit and should evaluate an individual-risk.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a subacute-pain then the clinician should avoid an increase above a diminishing-return-level.
If a clinician continues an opioid-therapy for a chronic-pain then the clinician should avoid an increase above a diminishing-return-level.
